{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018dsU73aNgjYXFnRPvwtv6M","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Slice 6b fix round 1 report (after codex's review)\n2\t\n3\tEngineer: claude-opus-5-5, 2026-10-13. Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6b`, branch\n4\t`srvm-phase6b`, on top of `c3d37586` (kept). **Nothing committed.** All lanes ran in the foreground on the\n5\tJVM. Node and Dart were not run.\n6\t\n7\t## Files changed\n8\t\n9\t| File | Change |\n10\t|---|---|\n11\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` | +151 / -39: lift discharge per context (F3); lower checks staged across the tree (F1, F2, F4) |\n12\t| `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` | +215 / -6: attach-count law (F5), 4 new deftests |\n13\t\n14\tNot edited: the design docs, `handoff.cljc`, `semantic_register.cljc`, `engine.cljc`, the audit allowlist.\n15\t\n16\t## Per finding\n17\t\n18\t### F1 (High): carrier refusals before attachment\n19\t\n20\t`check-task!` is split into four steps. `lower-task` runs **each step over the whole task tree before the\n21\tnext** (§3.3):\n22\t\n23\t1. `check-stores!` (task context, beside X1): no key of the root store or any module store is a reserved\n24\t   name → `:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name`, `:yin.k/role :store-key`. Before this fix, a module-store\n25\t   `yin/def` was not refused at all. Restore builds module stores with `into {}`, not `store-put`, so the\n26\t   lowered machine silently held `{host.mod {yin/def 42 …}}` (mutant A's log shows the machine).\n27\t2. `check-pendings!` (step 8): see F2.\n28\t3. `check-dependencies!` (step 9): requires/code agreement, trees, then **every carried vector must load\n29\t   in the receiver's kernel under its A** (`attaches?` = `module/attach-module` on the receiver,\n30\t   discarded; a vector whose address is not its key is refused too) → `:segment`. Then each closure row,\n31\t   via `check-closure!`, must name a `:closure` tuple of its vector with that entry (`:closure-origin`)\n32\t   and the tuple's binders (`:closure-binders`). These are the same conditions `lower-closure` enforces\n33\t   late. `:prim` names are checked after that, as before.\n34\t4. `check-custody!` (step 10).\n35\t\n36\tTests (`carrier-defects-refuse-before-attachment`, each asserting 0 attaches): root store `yin/def`\n37\t(codex's recipe), module store `yin/def` (row 18), closure entry `999999999` (codex's recipe),\n38\tclosure binders changed.\n39\t\n40\t### F2 (High): install records validated before attachment\n41\t\n42\t`check-install!` runs in step 8 for every install record:\n43\t- phase ∈ `#{:running :parked}` → else `:install-phase`;\n44\t- retained response: a map, `:status :ok`, manifest naming this module, exports a collection of\n45\t  symbols, `:image` a map whose `:value` the receiver's kernel loads (the same validation `spawn-child`'s\n46\t  `load-vector` runs) → else `:install-response`;\n47\t- relation to the carried child: the child's kind must be `:blocked` → else `:install-child`. Reason: the\n48\t  engine routes a halted (or explicitly parked, hence halted) child to `link-install` in the sweep it\n49\t  halts in, and a running child is not at a safepoint, so a carried child is always blocked (see B1).\n50\t\n51\tTests (0 attaches each): response `{}` and phase `:bogus` (codex's recipes), image value `[]`, manifest\n52\tnaming `other.mod`, child kind `:halted`.\n53\t\n54\t### F3 (High): occurrence- and context-sensitive discharge\n55\t\n56\tThe lift's walk now records a **context** `{:a A :at pc :env E}` for every closure (its entry), every\n57\t`:act` (its pc) and every return frame (its pc). `:tail` states record none: they resume through K, and\n58\ttheir segment never runs again. After the walk, `discharge!` takes each context, finds the body holding\n59\t`:at` (`code/parse`, `code/body-index`), and checks every free occurrence (`scan/free-occurrences`) whose\n60\tbody is that body or nested in it. Resolution follows resolve-var's order:\n61\t1. reserved;\n62\t2. **that context's own E**;\n63\t3. **the one store `engine/active-store` selects for it**: the root store when E has no store-of,\n64\t   otherwise that module store only, with no ambient fallback. Both travel: the root store whole, and\n65\t   the module store through `store-of!` → `module-store-nodes`;\n66\t4. a primitive (host-state refused here, i.e. only when resolution reaches the primitive step);\n67\t5. an installing module's export;\n68\t6. the module registry (deviation 4's composition prerequisite, unchanged).\n69\t\n70\tRemoved: the union of all module-store keys, the global \"bound anywhere\" set, and the global free-env\n71\tanswer. A carried E already holds the free env it was created under (E2 relies on that).\n72\t\n73\tTests (`free-occurrences-are-answered-in-their-own-context`):\n74\t- `(fn [] ghost)` refuses `#{ghost}`;\n75\t- adding `:module-stores {unrelated {ghost 42}}` still refuses (codex's negative case);\n76\t- a closure routed to module store `scoped` that lacks `ghost` refuses even though the root store has\n77\t  it;\n78\t- the root store answers a top-level closure;\n79\t- reachable only through the store;\n80\t- another closure's E binding `ghost` answers nothing, while its own E does;\n81\t- reachable only through a module store that a routed closure reaches;\n82\t- reachable only through another closure's E.\n83\t\n84\tPositive scoped case: a routed closure with `scoped {ghost 42}` beside `unrelated {other 1}` lifts with\n85\t0 serves. Only `scoped` travels. It lowers with 0 attaches, and calling the lowered closure on the\n86\treceiver answers 42.\n87\t\n88\t### F4 (Medium): custody after pending/operations and dependencies\n89\t\n90\tCustody is step 4 of the staged loop above. Tests (`carrier-steps-run-in-order-over-the-tree`):\n91\t- custody alone gives `:unsupported :custody`;\n92\t- codex's combined defect (trees `{}` + custody) gives `:unsatisfied` with the trees;\n93\t- **across the tree**: the child's missing trees + the root's custody gives the child's `:unsatisfied`;\n94\t- the root's missing trees + an install record phase `:bogus` gives `:install-phase` (step 8 beats\n95\t  step 9).\n96\t\n97\t### F5 (Medium): harness assertions\n98\t\n99\t- `laws` (every quick row and all 89 matrix runs) now asserts **attaches = distinct stream identities**\n100\t  of the carrier. The count is taken right after `lower-task`, before the relift and drive.\n101\t- The same assertion is added to `install-rows-carry-the-child` and to the two-waiters test.\n102\t- New `a-stream-shared-across-the-tree-attaches-once`: row 10 with the link-request stream held by both\n103\t  the root's store and the install child's store. One identity, reached twice\n104\t  (`occurrences > identities`), attached once, and both lowered tasks hold the identical handle.\n105\t  - Neither the corpus's install children nor the two waiters actually share a stream occurrence. The two\n106\t    waiters share one cursor cell, hence one `:stream` occurrence. So this explicit case was needed.\n107\t  - In the corpus, the duplicates come from rows 7-9 and the captures: the link response stream appears\n108\t    in both the pending and the cursor-cell body.\n109\t- The closure-only-obligation and transitive cases are the F3 tests above.\n110\t\n111\t## Proof that the tests fail without the fixes\n112\t\n113\t`git show … > file` and `git checkout -- file` both needed approval in this session, so each fix was\n114\tmutated away in place. Each run was followed by `cp target/fix1/rows.fixed.cljc` and a `diff -q` check;\n115\tthe final source matches the backup. Logs: `target/fix1/m*.log`.\n116\t\n117\t| Mutant | Change | Result (quick harness) |\n118\t|---|---|---|\n119\t| A | drop `check-stores!` | killed: 4 failures (both store-key cases; attach observed) |\n120\t| B | skip `check-closure!` | killed: 4 failures (origin, binders) |\n121\t| C | skip `check-install!` | killed: 10 failures (all five install cases + the step-8-before-9 test) |\n122\t| D | custody first (the old F4 order) | killed: 4 failures |\n123\t| E | all steps per task instead of per step over the tree | killed: 2 failures (child trees vs root custody) |\n124\t| F | answer from the union of root + all module stores (the old F3 rule) | killed: 3 failures |\n125\t| G | answer from any context's E (the old global \"bound\") | killed: 1 failure |\n126\t| **M9** (codex) | discharge before the walk, later discharge removed | **killed**: 7 failures |\n127\t| **M6** (codex) | a second `lower-marker` in the already-present branch | **killed**: quick 15 `bare-rows` + 4 captures + 3 tail + shared-tree; slow matrix 61 failures |\n128\t\n129\t## Verification (final source)\n130\t\n131\t- kondo on the three files (rows, harness, audit): 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n132\t- `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n\n133\t  yin.vm.store-write-audit-test`: **49 tests, 1021 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n134\t- Same with `-i :slow`: **2 tests, 706 assertions, 0 failures** (the matrix gained the attach-count law).\n135\t- `clojure -M:test -e :slow -r 'yin\\.vm\\.(ucf|semantic-register)\\..*'`: **54 namespaces, 834 tests,\n136\t  18714 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n137\t- Node/Dart not run.\n138\t\n139\t## Codex's remarks on deviations 1-10, B1-B4, handoff: what I changed vs left\n140\t\n141\tChanged in code:\n142\t- **Dev 3** (custody position): moved to step 10 (F4).\n143\t- **Dev 5** (discharge implementation): rewritten (F3). Lift-only discharge scope is kept, as accepted.\n144\t- **Dev 9** (lower-side kinds): the late raw errors are gone for the reviewed cases. The new named kinds\n145\t  are `:reserved-name` (role `:store-key`), `:install-phase`, `:install-response`, `:install-child`,\n146\t  `:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`, and `:segment` for a vector that does not load under its A.\n147\t  These, together with the earlier `:role :custody :incomplete-install :kind :parked-id :requires\n148\t  :segment`, are what the Architect's refusal table would list.\n149\t- **B4**: the code already discharges after the walk. It is now enforced by tests (M9).\n150\t\n151\tLeft for the Architect (design text, not code): dev 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8, 10 wording; B1 (rows 10/11\n152\trewrite; note my `:install-child` check encodes \"a carried child is blocked\", which an Architect-defined\n153\treachable kind-`:parked` child would have to relax); B2; B3 (`:row-shape` wording, and the emitter's\n154\textra-slot truncation, a 6a matter); the \"89 labelled executions, not 89 unique states\" wording.\n155\t\n156\t**handoff.cljc**: I withdraw my claim. Codex showed `(symbol (str (name m) \"/\" (name x)))` yields a\n157\tqualified symbol equal to `'host.mod/f`. Not a bug; no edit.\n158\t\n159\t## Uncertain / worth a reviewer's eye\n160\t\n161\t- **Context precision.** An occurrence is checked in every context whose body encloses it, including\n162\t  occurrences earlier in the same body than an `:act`'s pc (they cannot run again). This is conservative\n163\t  and can over-refuse only in a body with both a branch-skipped occurrence and an earlier-pc read.\n164\t  Nothing in the corpus hits it.\n165\t- **Uncarried code.** Code a context cannot reach at all, such as a segment carried only by a `:tail`,\n166\t  creates no obligations. That is deliberate.\n167\t- **Names the code defines itself.** A name that the carried code itself `def`s later is still refused\n168\t  unless it is already in the store, as before.\n169\t- **`attaches?` cost.** It re-validates every carried vector once per lower, plus each install image.\n170\t  That is cheap at corpus size.\n171\t- **Install image vs child code.** I did not require that the install image's A be among the child's\n172\t  carried segments. A child blocked after tail calls need not reference it.\n173\t- **Registry prerequisite.** Free-name obligations answered by the source registry are still not\n174\t  re-checked against the receiver (deviation 4/5, accepted as composition prerequisites).\n175\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cf2c11fe-d429-4593-ac23-eac8d35e2316","uuid":"897a7dea-2b64-4d2e-9b14-b5a5b98c5b06","timestamp":"2026-10-12T17:03:47.219Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6b/collab/1792290000000-engineer-6b-fix1.report.md","content":"# Slice 6b fix round 1 report (after codex's review)\n\nEngineer: claude-opus-5-5, 2026-10-13. Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6b`, branch\n`srvm-phase6b`, on top of `c3d37586` (kept). **Nothing committed.** All lanes ran in the foreground on the\nJVM. Node and Dart were not run.\n\n## Files changed\n\n| File | Change |\n|---|---|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` | +151 / -39: lift discharge per context (F3); lower checks staged across the tree (F1, F2, F4) |\n| `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` | +215 / -6: attach-count law (F5), 4 new deftests |\n\nNot edited: the design docs, `handoff.cljc`, `semantic_register.cljc`, `engine.cljc`, the audit allowlist.\n\n## Per finding\n\n### F1 (High): carrier refusals before attachment\n\n`check-task!` is split into four steps. `lower-task` runs **each step over the whole task tree before the\nnext** (§3.3):\n\n1. `check-stores!` (task context, beside X1): no key of the root store or any module store is a reserved\n   name → `:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name`, `:yin.k/role :store-key`. Before this fix, a module-store\n   `yin/def` was not refused at all. Restore builds module stores with `into {}`, not `store-put`, so the\n   lowered machine silently held `{host.mod {yin/def 42 …}}` (mutant A's log shows the machine).\n2. `check-pendings!` (step 8): see F2.\n3. `check-dependencies!` (step 9): requires/code agreement, trees, then **every carried vector must load\n   in the receiver's kernel under its A** (`attaches?` = `module/attach-module` on the receiver,\n   discarded; a vector whose address is not its key is refused too) → `:segment`. Then each closure row,\n   via `check-closure!`, must name a `:closure` tuple of its vector with that entry (`:closure-origin`)\n   and the tuple's binders (`:closure-binders`). These are the same conditions `lower-closure` enforces\n   late. `:prim` names are checked after that, as before.\n4. `check-custody!` (step 10).\n\nTests (`carrier-defects-refuse-before-attachment`, each asserting 0 attaches): root store `yin/def`\n(codex's recipe), module store `yin/def` (row 18), closure entry `999999999` (codex's recipe),\nclosure binders changed.\n\n### F2 (High): install records validated before attachment\n\n`check-install!` runs in step 8 for every install record:\n- phase ∈ `#{:running :parked}` → else `:install-phase`;\n- retained response: a map, `:status :ok`, manifest naming this module, exports a collection of\n  symbols, `:image` a map whose `:value` the receiver's kernel loads (the same validation `spawn-child`'s\n  `load-vector` runs) → else `:install-response`;\n- relation to the carried child: the child's kind must be `:blocked` → else `:install-child`. Reason: the\n  engine routes a halted (or explicitly parked, hence halted) child to `link-install` in the sweep it\n  halts in, and a running child is not at a safepoint, so a carried child is always blocked (see B1).\n\nTests (0 attaches each): response `{}` and phase `:bogus` (codex's recipes), image value `[]`, manifest\nnaming `other.mod`, child kind `:halted`.\n\n### F3 (High): occurrence- and context-sensitive discharge\n\nThe lift's walk now records a **context** `{:a A :at pc :env E}` for every closure (its entry), every\n`:act` (its pc) and every return frame (its pc). `:tail` states record none: they resume through K, and\ntheir segment never runs again. After the walk, `discharge!` takes each context, finds the body holding\n`:at` (`code/parse`, `code/body-index`), and checks every free occurrence (`scan/free-occurrences`) whose\nbody is that body or nested in it. Resolution follows resolve-var's order:\n1. reserved;\n2. **that context's own E**;\n3. **the one store `engine/active-store` selects for it**: the root store when E has no store-of,\n   otherwise that module store only, with no ambient fallback. Both travel: the root store whole, and\n   the module store through `store-of!` → `module-store-nodes`;\n4. a primitive (host-state refused here, i.e. only when resolution reaches the primitive step);\n5. an installing module's export;\n6. the module registry (deviation 4's composition prerequisite, unchanged).\n\nRemoved: the union of all module-store keys, the global \"bound anywhere\" set, and the global free-env\nanswer. A carried E already holds the free env it was created under (E2 relies on that).\n\nTests (`free-occurrences-are-answered-in-their-own-context`):\n- `(fn [] ghost)` refuses `#{ghost}`;\n- adding `:module-stores {unrelated {ghost 42}}` still refuses (codex's negative case);\n- a closure routed to module store `scoped` that lacks `ghost` refuses even though the root store has\n  it;\n- the root store answers a top-level closure;\n- reachable only through the store;\n- another closure's E binding `ghost` answers nothing, while its own E does;\n- reachable only through a module store that a routed closure reaches;\n- reachable only through another closure's E.\n\nPositive scoped case: a routed closure with `scoped {ghost 42}` beside `unrelated {other 1}` lifts with\n0 serves. Only `scoped` travels. It lowers with 0 attaches, and calling the lowered closure on the\nreceiver answers 42.\n\n### F4 (Medium): custody after pending/operations and dependencies\n\nCustody is step 4 of the staged loop above. Tests (`carrier-steps-run-in-order-over-the-tree`):\n- custody alone gives `:unsupported :custody`;\n- codex's combined defect (trees `{}` + custody) gives `:unsatisfied` with the trees;\n- **across the tree**: the child's missing trees + the root's custody gives the child's `:unsatisfied`;\n- the root's missing trees + an install record phase `:bogus` gives `:install-phase` (step 8 beats\n  step 9).\n\n### F5 (Medium): harness assertions\n\n- `laws` (every quick row and all 89 matrix runs) now asserts **attaches = distinct stream identities**\n  of the carrier. The count is taken right after `lower-task`, before the relift and drive.\n- The same assertion is added to `install-rows-carry-the-child` and to the two-waiters test.\n- New `a-stream-shared-across-the-tree-attaches-once`: row 10 with the link-request stream held by both\n  the root's store and the install child's store. One identity, reached twice\n  (`occurrences > identities`), attached once, and both lowered tasks hold the identical handle.\n  - Neither the corpus's install children nor the two waiters actually share a stream occurrence. The two\n    waiters share one cursor cell, hence one `:stream` occurrence. So this explicit case was needed.\n  - In the corpus, the duplicates come from rows 7-9 and the captures: the link response stream appears\n    in both the pending and the cursor-cell body.\n- The closure-only-obligation and transitive cases are the F3 tests above.\n\n## Proof that the tests fail without the fixes\n\n`git show … > file` and `git checkout -- file` both needed approval in this session, so each fix was\nmutated away in place. Each run was followed by `cp target/fix1/rows.fixed.cljc` and a `diff -q` check;\nthe final source matches the backup. Logs: `target/fix1/m*.log`.\n\n| Mutant | Change | Result (quick harness) |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | drop `check-stores!` | killed: 4 failures (both store-key cases; attach observed) |\n| B | skip `check-closure!` | killed: 4 failures (origin, binders) |\n| C | skip `check-install!` | killed: 10 failures (all five install cases + the step-8-before-9 test) |\n| D | custody first (the old F4 order) | killed: 4 failures |\n| E | all steps per task instead of per step over the tree | killed: 2 failures (child trees vs root custody) |\n| F | answer from the union of root + all module stores (the old F3 rule) | killed: 3 failures |\n| G | answer from any context's E (the old global \"bound\") | killed: 1 failure |\n| **M9** (codex) | discharge before the walk, later discharge removed | **killed**: 7 failures |\n| **M6** (codex) | a second `lower-marker` in the already-present branch | **killed**: quick 15 `bare-rows` + 4 captures + 3 tail + shared-tree; slow matrix 61 failures |\n\n## Verification (final source)\n\n- kondo on the three files (rows, harness, audit): 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n\n  yin.vm.store-write-audit-test`: **49 tests, 1021 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n- Same with `-i :slow`: **2 tests, 706 assertions, 0 failures** (the matrix gained the attach-count law).\n- `clojure -M:test -e :slow -r 'yin\\.vm\\.(ucf|semantic-register)\\..*'`: **54 namespaces, 834 tests,\n  18714 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n- Node/Dart not run.\n\n## Codex's remarks on deviations 1-10, B1-B4, handoff: what I changed vs left\n\nChanged in code:\n- **Dev 3** (custody position): moved to step 10 (F4).\n- **Dev 5** (discharge implementation): rewritten (F3). Lift-only discharge scope is kept, as accepted.\n- **Dev 9** (lower-side kinds): the late raw errors are gone for the reviewed cases. The new named kinds\n  are `:reserved-name` (role `:store-key`), `:install-phase`, `:install-response`, `:install-child`,\n  `:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`, and `:segment` for a vector that does not load under its A.\n  These, together with the earlier `:role :custody :incomplete-install :kind :parked-id :requires\n  :segment`, are what the Architect's refusal table would list.\n- **B4**: the code already discharges after the walk. It is now enforced by tests (M9).\n\nLeft for the Architect (design text, not code): dev 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8, 10 wording; B1 (rows 10/11\nrewrite; note my `:install-child` check encodes \"a carried child is blocked\", which an Architect-defined\nreachable kind-`:parked` child would have to relax); B2; B3 (`:row-shape` wording, and the emitter's\nextra-slot truncation, a 6a matter); the \"89 labelled executions, not 89 unique states\" wording.\n\n**handoff.cljc**: I withdraw my claim. Codex showed `(symbol (str (name m) \"/\" (name x)))` yields a\nqualified symbol equal to `'host.mod/f`. Not a bug; no edit.\n\n## Uncertain / worth a reviewer's eye\n\n- **Context precision.** An occurrence is checked in every context whose body encloses it, including\n  occurrences earlier in the same body than an `:act`'s pc (they cannot run again). This is conservative\n  and can over-refuse only in a body with both a branch-skipped occurrence and an earlier-pc read.\n  Nothing in the corpus hits it.\n- **Uncarried code.** Code a context cannot reach at all, such as a segment carried only by a `:tail`,\n  creates no obligations. That is deliberate.\n- **Names the code defines itself.** A name that the carried code itself `def`s later is still refused\n  unless it is already in the store, as before.\n- **`attaches?` cost.** It re-validates every carried vector once per lower, plus each install image.\n  That is cheap at corpus size.\n- **Install image vs child code.** I did not require that the install image's A be among the child's\n  carried segments. A child blocked after tail calls need not reference it.\n- **Registry prerequisite.** Free-name obligations answered by the source registry are still not\n  re-checked against the receiver (deviation 4/5, accepted as composition prerequisites).\n","numLines":175,"startLine":1,"totalLines":175}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018dsU73aNgjYXFnRPvwtv6M","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
