sal is\n`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/stores #{mid …}}`. This\nmatches the shape of the missing-trees refusal in the same step.\n\nTests (`every-store-of-names-a-module-store-of-its-own-task`). Each refusal also asserts **0 attaches**:\n- codex's recipe: a closure routed to `scoped {ghost 42}` and a stream in the root store. The intact\n  carrier attaches once and `(f)` returns 42. After `(assoc-in task [:slots 7] [])` and `rows/emit`, lower\n  refuses with `#{scoped}`.\n- the same with a **present empty store** `scoped {}` and `(fn [] 7)`. The intact carrier attaches once\n  and returns 7. Stripping its rows refuses.\n- **frame E**: row 17 (act E routed to host.mod's store) with slot 7 emptied refuses with one missing\n  store.\n- **child**: row 10, with the root's and the child's wait E both routed to `scoped {}`. Both tasks carry\n  `scoped`, and the intact carrier lowers to a blocked machine with the law's attach count. Emptying only\n  the **child's** slot 7 refuses with `#{scoped}`, even though the parent carries a store with that key.\n\n## F6 (Medium): discharge from each context's resume pc, by control flow\n\n`encloses?` is gone. The new `live-pcs [v at]` is public, because ClojureDart cannot var-quote a\nprivate fn. It is a worklist over `analysis/successors` (§8.1's edges, so a backward jump makes earlier\ncode live). For every `:closure` it reaches, it also adds that closure's body-pc, because reachable code\ncan still construct the closure, and the body's nested closures follow transitively. `discharge!` then\nchecks only the free occurrences whose pc is in `live-pcs` of the context's `(a, at)`. The answering rule\nis unchanged: the context's own E, then the one active store, then primitive, installing export,\nregistry.\n\nTests (`discharge-follows-control-flow-from-the-resume-pc`):\n- codex's `(if false ghost (next! (cursor (stream/make 4))))` lifts. The receiver attaches once, and\n  after the source stream is fed 73, **both source and receiver complete with 73**. This holds without\n  the unrelated store, with `:module-stores {unrelated {ghost 42}}`, and with `(if false (fn [] ghost) …)`\n  (a closure that can no longer be constructed).\n- negative: `((fn [p q] 1) (next! …) ghost)` refuses `#{ghost}` (the read comes after the resume pc in\n  the same body).\n- negative: `((fn [p q] 1) (next! …) (fn [] ghost))` refuses `#{ghost}`. The closure is constructed\n  after the resume pc, so its body counts.\n- **backward jump**: §3.1 parsing refuses any backward jump (I checked: `code/well-formed?` gives\n  `:not-expression-structured` for one), so no carried vector can contain one, and an end-to-end\n  transport case cannot be built. Instead the test calls `live-pcs` directly on\n  `[[:const 1 false] [:branch-false 1 4] [:var 0 ghost] [:jump 6] [:const 0 1] [:jump 2] [:halt 0]]`.\n  From pc 4, pc 2 (`ghost`) is live via the backward `:jump 2`. From pc 3 it is not.\n\nThe existing per-context negatives (`free-occurrences-are-answered-in-their-own-context`) all still\npass.\n\n## Attach-count law: a check outside `laws`\n\n`bare-rows-match-the-reference` now sums attaches and distinct stream identities over the corpus. It\nasserts that the sum of attaches is **non-zero** and that the two sums are equal. Replacing the\nassertion in `laws` with `(is true …)` changes no behavior, so on its own the mutant cannot fail any\ntest. What matters is that the attach count is still asserted somewhere after that edit. Combined with a\nproduction duplicate-attach mutant, it now fails at this new assertion and at the shared-tree test (see\nbelow).\n\n## Mutants (quick harness, each applied alone, restored by `cp` + byte compare)\n\nScript and logs: `target/fix2/mutants.py`, `target/fix2/<mutant>.log`. Final sources are byte-identical\nto `target/fix2/*.fixed.cljc`.\n\n| Mutant | Change | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | check every occurrence of the segment (no reachability; stricter than HEAD's `encloses?`) | killed: 3 errors (all three resumed-pc transports) |\n| B | `live-pcs` without closure bodies | killed: 1 failure (constructible closure) |\n| C | numeric filter: `live-pcs` = `(range at (count v))` | killed: 1 failure (backward jump) + 1 error (dead closure) |\n| D (codex) | restore union discharge | killed: 3 failures |\n| E | drop the store census | killed: 9 failures (every F7 refusal) |\n| G | census over closures only, not `:env` rows | killed: 5 failures (row 17, child) |\n| codex | drop `check-stores!` | killed: 4 failures |\n| codex | drop `check-pendings!` | killed: 11 failures |\n| codex | drop `check-dependencies!` | killed: 21 failures |\n| codex | drop `check-custody!` | killed: 2 failures |\n| codex + | `laws` count → `(is true …)` **and** a duplicate `lower-marker` | killed: 2 failures (new corpus check, shared-tree test) |\n\nF6 tests against HEAD's code: under HEAD, `rows/live-pcs` does not exist, so the namespace does not\ncompile. Mutant A stands in for the old rule semantically, and codex's union mutant D also reproduces\nF6's old acceptance.\n\n## Verification (final source)\n\n- kondo on rows and harness: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n\n  yin.vm.store-write-audit-test`: **51 tests, 1049 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n- Same with `-i :slow`: **2 tests, 706 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n- `clojure -M:test -e :slow -r 'yin\\.vm\\.(ucf|semantic-register)\\..*'`: **836 tests, 18742 assertions,\n  0 failures, 0 errors**.\n- Node/Dart not run.\n\n## Worth a reviewer's eye\n\n- **Refusal kind for F7.** I chose `:unsatisfied`/`:incomplete` with `:yin.k/stores`, which matches how\n  missing trees are reported. If the Architect's refusal table treats a store-of with no row as a\n  malformed carrier, the alternative is `:undecodable`. The change would be one line.\n- **`live-pcs` is public** only so the backward-jump property can be tested portably.\n- **Context pc semantics.** `at` is the pc the context resumes at (an act's or frame's pc, a closure's\n  entry), and it is included in the live set. If some context's `:pc` were ever the pc of an instruction\n  that already completed, including it would only make the check stricter, never looser.\n- The child's census is its own slot 7. Restore also builds each child's module stores from its own\n  rows, so the check and restore agree.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XCnyWxURPKHV6r9xFVio9n","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
